    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old male named Raymond with moderate dementia is brought to the emergency department with second-degree burns on the bilateral lower feet and ankles. On physical examination, you note a sharp horizontal line of demarcation (''stocking'' distribution) with sparing of the skin creases on the anterior ankles and posterior knees. The caregiver states, ''Raymond stepped into a hot bath he drew hisself.'' What does this burn pattern indicate?', '["Accidental splash burn; which typically presents with random, asymmetrical drop-like burn marks across the thighs and abdomen", "Forced immersion burn; the stocking distribution and flexural sparing indicate the patient''s feet were held down in hot water, contradicting the caregiver''s story; this is physical abuse and requires emergency admission and reporting", "Severe diabetic neuropathy; which causes spontaneous blistering (bullosis diabeticorum) resembling thermal immersion burns", "Normal age-related thermal sensitivity; because elderly skin burns at much lower temperatures than younger skin during self-bathing", "Necrotizing vasculitis; a systemic autoimmune condition presenting with cutaneous necrosis resembling thermal burns"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A **forced immersion burn** is characterized by a symmetric, uniform distribution (often a stocking or glove pattern) with a **sharp horizontal level of demarcation** and **sparing of flexure lines** (like the skin folds behind the knees or ankles, caused by the patient flexing the joints to resist the hot water). Accidental burns, in contrast, show asymmetrical, irregular splash marks. This pattern strongly indicates the patient was held down in scalding water, representing severe physical abuse. Emergency hospitalization is required for burn care, safety, and mandatory reporting. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old male named Stanley with moderate dementia is brought to the emergency department with second-degree burns on the bilateral lower feet and ankles. On physical examination, you note a sharp horizontal line of demarcation (''stocking'' distribution) with sparing of the skin creases on the anterior ankles and posterior knees. The caregiver states, ''Stanley stepped into a hot bath he drew hisself.'' What does this burn pattern indicate?', '["Accidental splash burn; which typically presents with random, asymmetrical drop-like burn marks across the thighs and abdomen", "Normal age-related thermal sensitivity; because elderly skin burns at much lower temperatures than younger skin during self-bathing", "Severe diabetic neuropathy; which causes spontaneous blistering (bullosis diabeticorum) resembling thermal immersion burns", "Forced immersion burn; the stocking distribution and flexural sparing indicate the patient''s feet were held down in hot water, contradicting the caregiver''s story; this is physical abuse and requires emergency admission and reporting", "Necrotizing vasculitis; a systemic autoimmune condition presenting with cutaneous necrosis resembling thermal burns"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A **forced immersion burn** is characterized by a symmetric, uniform distribution (often a stocking or glove pattern) with a **sharp horizontal level of demarcation** and **sparing of flexure lines** (like the skin folds behind the knees or ankles, caused by the patient flexing the joints to resist the hot water). Accidental burns, in contrast, show asymmetrical, irregular splash marks. This pattern strongly indicates the patient was held down in scalding water, representing severe physical abuse. Emergency hospitalization is required for burn care, safety, and mandatory reporting. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
