    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old male named Donald with moderate dementia is brought to the emergency department with second-degree burns on the bilateral lower feet and ankles. On physical examination, you note a sharp horizontal line of demarcation (''stocking'' distribution) with sparing of the skin creases on the anterior ankles and posterior knees. The caregiver states, ''Donald stepped into a hot bath he drew hisself.'' What does this burn pattern indicate?', '["Severe diabetic neuropathy; which causes spontaneous blistering (bullosis diabeticorum) resembling thermal immersion burns", "Necrotizing vasculitis; a systemic autoimmune condition presenting with cutaneous necrosis resembling thermal burns", "Forced immersion burn; the stocking distribution and flexural sparing indicate the patient''s feet were held down in hot water, contradicting the caregiver''s story; this is physical abuse and requires emergency admission and reporting", "Normal age-related thermal sensitivity; because elderly skin burns at much lower temperatures than younger skin during self-bathing", "Accidental splash burn; which typically presents with random, asymmetrical drop-like burn marks across the thighs and abdomen"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A **forced immersion burn** is characterized by a symmetric, uniform distribution (often a stocking or glove pattern) with a **sharp horizontal level of demarcation** and **sparing of flexure lines** (like the skin folds behind the knees or ankles, caused by the patient flexing the joints to resist the hot water). Accidental burns, in contrast, show asymmetrical, irregular splash marks. This pattern strongly indicates the patient was held down in scalding water, representing severe physical abuse. Emergency hospitalization is required for burn care, safety, and mandatory reporting. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old male named Edwin with advanced dementia is completely dependent on his adult son for all activities of daily living. The patient is brought to the clinic and found to have a weight loss of 18 lbs (10% of body weight) in the past 5 months, severe skin turgor loss, dry mucous membranes, and laboratory values showing a serum sodium of 160 mEq/L and a BUN/creatinine ratio of 35:1. The caregiver states, ''I feed Edwin three times a day, but he just refuses to eat.'' What is the most appropriate diagnostic and management strategy?', '["Immediately place a percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy (PEG) feeding tube, as this is the standard treatment for malnutrition in advanced dementia", "Accept the caregiver''s explanation and prescribe high-dose oral loop diuretics to manage the patient''s hypernatremia", "Reassure the caregiver that severe dehydration and rapid weight loss are normal and beneficial phases of late-stage dementia", "Evaluate for caregiver neglect; review food intake logs, assess for dysphagia, check pharmacy records for missed meds, and file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS) due to severe malnutrition and dehydration", "Diagnose primary anorexia nervosa and refer the patient for inpatient psychiatric eating disorder treatment"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Severe malnutrition, rapid weight loss, and hypernatremic dehydration in a dependent older adult are significant red flags for caregiver neglect (either active withholding or passive inability to feed). The clinician must evaluate the situation by: (1) ruling out medical causes (e.g., severe dysphagia, cancer), (2) interviewing the patient and caregiver, (3) checking pharmacy refills, and (4) filing a report with Adult Protective Services (APS). In advanced dementia, hand-feeding should be optimized; PEG tubes are not recommended as they do not improve nutritional parameters or prolong life. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14 & Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
