    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old male named Robert with advanced dementia is completely dependent on his adult son for all activities of daily living. The patient is brought to the clinic and found to have a weight loss of 22 lbs (10% of body weight) in the past 6 months, severe skin turgor loss, dry mucous membranes, and laboratory values showing a serum sodium of 158 mEq/L and a BUN/creatinine ratio of 35:1. The caregiver states, ''I feed Robert three times a day, but he just refuses to eat.'' What is the most appropriate diagnostic and management strategy?', '["Reassure the caregiver that severe dehydration and rapid weight loss are normal and beneficial phases of late-stage dementia", "Immediately place a percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy (PEG) feeding tube, as this is the standard treatment for malnutrition in advanced dementia", "Diagnose primary anorexia nervosa and refer the patient for inpatient psychiatric eating disorder treatment", "Evaluate for caregiver neglect; review food intake logs, assess for dysphagia, check pharmacy records for missed meds, and file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS) due to severe malnutrition and dehydration", "Accept the caregiver''s explanation and prescribe high-dose oral loop diuretics to manage the patient''s hypernatremia"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Severe malnutrition, rapid weight loss, and hypernatremic dehydration in a dependent older adult are significant red flags for caregiver neglect (either active withholding or passive inability to feed). The clinician must evaluate the situation by: (1) ruling out medical causes (e.g., severe dysphagia, cancer), (2) interviewing the patient and caregiver, (3) checking pharmacy refills, and (4) filing a report with Adult Protective Services (APS). In advanced dementia, hand-feeding should be optimized; PEG tubes are not recommended as they do not improve nutritional parameters or prolong life. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14 & Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
