    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old male named John with advanced dementia is completely dependent on his grandson for all activities of daily living. The patient is brought to the clinic and found to have a weight loss of 18 lbs (10% of body weight) in the past 5 months, severe skin turgor loss, dry mucous membranes, and laboratory values showing a serum sodium of 155 mEq/L and a BUN/creatinine ratio of 40:1. The caregiver states, ''I feed John three times a day, but he just refuses to eat.'' What is the most appropriate diagnostic and management strategy?', '["Accept the caregiver''s explanation and prescribe high-dose oral loop diuretics to manage the patient''s hypernatremia", "Reassure the caregiver that severe dehydration and rapid weight loss are normal and beneficial phases of late-stage dementia", "Evaluate for caregiver neglect; review food intake logs, assess for dysphagia, check pharmacy records for missed meds, and file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS) due to severe malnutrition and dehydration", "Diagnose primary anorexia nervosa and refer the patient for inpatient psychiatric eating disorder treatment", "Immediately place a percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy (PEG) feeding tube, as this is the standard treatment for malnutrition in advanced dementia"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Severe malnutrition, rapid weight loss, and hypernatremic dehydration in a dependent older adult are significant red flags for caregiver neglect (either active withholding or passive inability to feed). The clinician must evaluate the situation by: (1) ruling out medical causes (e.g., severe dysphagia, cancer), (2) interviewing the patient and caregiver, (3) checking pharmacy refills, and (4) filing a report with Adult Protective Services (APS). In advanced dementia, hand-feeding should be optimized; PEG tubes are not recommended as they do not improve nutritional parameters or prolong life. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14 & Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old male named Kenneth with mild cognitive decline is brought to the clinic by his adult son because the patient has been ''sleeping all day'' and is increasingly unsteady on his feet, resulting in a fall. On examination, the patient is extremely lethargic, slurring words, and has a positive Romberg sign. A serum toxicology screen is positive for alprazolam, which has never been prescribed to the patient. The caregiver admits, ''Sometimes I give Kenneth a little of my alprazolam to keep him quiet so I can get some housework done.'' What is the clinical term for this practice, and what is the clinician''s obligation?', '["Passive caregiver neglect; since the caregiver did not intend to poison the patient, no reporting is required", "Accidental drug interaction; advise the patient to separate their medications by at least 2 hours to prevent cross-contamination", "Self-neglect; because the patient should have refused to swallow the pills given by the caregiver", "Chemical restraint (elder abuse); administering sedating medications without clinical indication or consent for behavioral control; file a mandatory report with Adult Protective Services (APS)", "Appropriate off-label sedation; document the caregiver''s rationale and officially write a prescription for the sedative to ensure safety"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A **chemical restraint** is the use of any medication (typically benzodiazepines, antipsychotics, or sedating antihistamines) to restrict a patient''s freedom of movement or control their behavior, where the medication is not standard treatment for the patient''s medical or psychiatric condition. Toking or sedating an older adult for the caregiver''s convenience is a form of elder abuse/neglect. The clinician must report this suspected abuse to Adult Protective Services (APS) and address the safety of the patient''s home environment. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
