    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old male named Richard with mild cognitive decline is brought to the clinic by his grandson because the patient has been ''sleeping all day'' and is increasingly unsteady on his feet, resulting in a fall. On examination, the patient is extremely lethargic, slurring words, and has a positive Romberg sign. A serum toxicology screen is positive for diazepam, which has never been prescribed to the patient. The caregiver admits, ''Sometimes I give Richard a little of my diazepam to keep him quiet so I can get some housework done.'' What is the clinical term for this practice, and what is the clinician''s obligation?', '["Chemical restraint (elder abuse); administering sedating medications without clinical indication or consent for behavioral control; file a mandatory report with Adult Protective Services (APS)", "Accidental drug interaction; advise the patient to separate their medications by at least 2 hours to prevent cross-contamination", "Appropriate off-label sedation; document the caregiver''s rationale and officially write a prescription for the sedative to ensure safety", "Self-neglect; because the patient should have refused to swallow the pills given by the caregiver", "Passive caregiver neglect; since the caregiver did not intend to poison the patient, no reporting is required"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A **chemical restraint** is the use of any medication (typically benzodiazepines, antipsychotics, or sedating antihistamines) to restrict a patient''s freedom of movement or control their behavior, where the medication is not standard treatment for the patient''s medical or psychiatric condition. Toking or sedating an older adult for the caregiver''s convenience is a form of elder abuse/neglect. The clinician must report this suspected abuse to Adult Protective Services (APS) and address the safety of the patient''s home environment. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 92-year-old male named Carl presents to the clinic with his daughter for evaluation of a skin lesion. During the intake, the nurse notes that the daughter is highly defensive, aggressive, and answers all questions directed at Carl. When the clinician asks the daughter to step out of the room for a brief patient exam, the daughter refuses, stating, ''Carl is confused and needs me here for everything. I refuse to leave.'' Which of the following is the most appropriate action for the clinical team?', '["Acquiesce to the caregiver''s demand and conduct the interview in their presence to avoid escalating the conflict", "Cancel the medical visit immediately and tell the patient they cannot receive care unless they return alone", "State firmly that a private examination is a standard clinic safety policy, enlist clinic security or administration to assist in escorting the caregiver out, and interview the patient alone about potential abuse and neglect", "Immediately call the police to arrest the caregiver for obstruction of justice and assault", "Conduct the physical exam with the caregiver present but use written notes to ask the patient if they are being abused"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Caregiver behavioral red flags include defensiveness, hostility, answering for the patient, and refusing to allow the patient to be interviewed alone. Interviewing the patient alone is a **critical clinical standard** when screening for elder abuse or domestic violence. If a caregiver refuses to leave, the clinician must firmly state that private interviews are standard policy, use clinic staff or security to separate them, and proceed with the private safety screening. Yielding to the caregiver prevents the patient from disclosing abuse out of fear of retaliation. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old female named Martha presents to the clinic with her niece for evaluation of a skin lesion. During the intake, the nurse notes that the niece is highly defensive, aggressive, and answers all questions directed at Martha. When the clinician asks the niece to step out of the room for a brief patient exam, the niece refuses, stating, ''Martha is confused and needs me here for everything. I refuse to leave.'' Which of the following is the most appropriate action for the clinical team?', '["State firmly that a private examination is a standard clinic safety policy, enlist clinic security or administration to assist in escorting the caregiver out, and interview the patient alone about potential abuse and neglect", "Cancel the medical visit immediately and tell the patient they cannot receive care unless they return alone", "Immediately call the police to arrest the caregiver for obstruction of justice and assault", "Conduct the physical exam with the caregiver present but use written notes to ask the patient if they are being abused", "Acquiesce to the caregiver''s demand and conduct the interview in their presence to avoid escalating the conflict"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Caregiver behavioral red flags include defensiveness, hostility, answering for the patient, and refusing to allow the patient to be interviewed alone. Interviewing the patient alone is a **critical clinical standard** when screening for elder abuse or domestic violence. If a caregiver refuses to leave, the clinician must firmly state that private interviews are standard policy, use clinic staff or security to separate them, and proceed with the private safety screening. Yielding to the caregiver prevents the patient from disclosing abuse out of fear of retaliation. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
