    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old female named Gladys presents to the clinic with her daughter for evaluation of a skin lesion. During the intake, the nurse notes that the daughter is highly defensive, aggressive, and answers all questions directed at Gladys. When the clinician asks the daughter to step out of the room for a brief patient exam, the daughter refuses, stating, ''Gladys is confused and needs me here for everything. I refuse to leave.'' Which of the following is the most appropriate action for the clinical team?', '["Conduct the physical exam with the caregiver present but use written notes to ask the patient if they are being abused", "State firmly that a private examination is a standard clinic safety policy, enlist clinic security or administration to assist in escorting the caregiver out, and interview the patient alone about potential abuse and neglect", "Cancel the medical visit immediately and tell the patient they cannot receive care unless they return alone", "Acquiesce to the caregiver''s demand and conduct the interview in their presence to avoid escalating the conflict", "Immediately call the police to arrest the caregiver for obstruction of justice and assault"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Caregiver behavioral red flags include defensiveness, hostility, answering for the patient, and refusing to allow the patient to be interviewed alone. Interviewing the patient alone is a **critical clinical standard** when screening for elder abuse or domestic violence. If a caregiver refuses to leave, the clinician must firmly state that private interviews are standard policy, use clinic staff or security to separate them, and proceed with the private safety screening. Yielding to the caregiver prevents the patient from disclosing abuse out of fear of retaliation. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
