    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old male named Kenneth presents to the clinic with his daughter for evaluation of a skin lesion. During the intake, the nurse notes that the daughter is highly defensive, aggressive, and answers all questions directed at Kenneth. When the clinician asks the daughter to step out of the room for a brief patient exam, the daughter refuses, stating, ''Kenneth is confused and needs me here for everything. I refuse to leave.'' Which of the following is the most appropriate action for the clinical team?', '["State firmly that a private examination is a standard clinic safety policy, enlist clinic security or administration to assist in escorting the caregiver out, and interview the patient alone about potential abuse and neglect", "Cancel the medical visit immediately and tell the patient they cannot receive care unless they return alone", "Acquiesce to the caregiver''s demand and conduct the interview in their presence to avoid escalating the conflict", "Conduct the physical exam with the caregiver present but use written notes to ask the patient if they are being abused", "Immediately call the police to arrest the caregiver for obstruction of justice and assault"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Caregiver behavioral red flags include defensiveness, hostility, answering for the patient, and refusing to allow the patient to be interviewed alone. Interviewing the patient alone is a **critical clinical standard** when screening for elder abuse or domestic violence. If a caregiver refuses to leave, the clinician must firmly state that private interviews are standard policy, use clinic staff or security to separate them, and proceed with the private safety screening. Yielding to the caregiver prevents the patient from disclosing abuse out of fear of retaliation. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female named Catherine with mild cognitive impairment is seen in the clinic. The clinician notes that when the adult son enters the room, the patient immediately stops speaking, tenses her shoulders, and looks down at the floor. When asked directly about a bruise on her forearm, the patient looks at the caregiver''s face before whispering, ''I bumped it on the door, it was my fault.'' The caregiver appears hostile and glares at the patient. What is the most appropriate interpretation of these behavioral cues?', '["The patient''s behavior is a typical manifestation of mild cognitive impairment and normal aging, requiring no further investigation", "The patient''s behavior is pathognomonic for a stroke, and the clinician should immediately activate the stroke protocol", "The patient''s behavior indicates fear and hypervigilance, which are key behavioral red flags for elder abuse; the clinician must separate the patient and caregiver and screen for safety", "The patient''s behavior indicates active depression, and the clinician should immediately start an SSRI without investigating the bruising", "The patient is displaying oppositional defiant behavior toward the caregiver, and the caregiver should be counseled on behavioral control"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Behavioral red flags in the patient include hypervigilance, sudden withdrawal or mutism in the presence of the caregiver, looking to the caregiver for permission to speak, and apologizing for injuries. These signs suggest fear of retaliation and control. The clinician must separate the patient and caregiver, conduct a private interview, and file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS) if abuse is suspected. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Lachs & Pillemer, N Engl J Med 2015; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
