    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old female named Ruby is bedbound due to severe dementia and resides at home. She is brought to the clinic and found to have a large, Stage IV sacral pressure injury that is 6 cm in diameter, filled with foul-smelling necrotic tissue, and covered in dried feces. The caregiver, her spouse, states they apply ointment once a week and didn''t notice the wound. The patient has had no professional home health care or nursing visits. What is the most appropriate clinical assessment and action?', '["Unilaterally report the patient to law enforcement for failing to comply with standard skin care recommendations", "Reassure the caregiver that pressure ulcers are an inevitable biological consequence of bedbound status and no intervention is required", "Diagnose self-neglect; because a bedbound patient is responsible for repositioning themselves every 2 hours to prevent ulcers", "Suspect severe caregiver neglect; admit the patient to the hospital for wound debridement, infection management, and safety, and file an immediate report with Adult Protective Services (APS)", "Prescribe oral antibiotics and send the patient home with the caregiver, instructing them to clean the wound daily with hydrogen peroxide"]'::jsonb, 3, 'An advanced, necrotic Stage IV pressure injury covered in stool in a completely dependent patient represents severe caregiver neglect. While pressure ulcers can occur despite good care, a wound of this severity that is unaddressed and lacks professional medical involvement indicates a failure to provide essential care (repositioning, hygiene, wound management). The patient requires hospitalization for wound care and workup for osteomyelitis/sepsis. A mandatory report to Adult Protective Services (APS) must be filed immediately. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14 & Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
