    (v_topic_id, 'A 96-year-old female named Jean with moderate Alzheimer''s disease is brought from an assisted living facility with new-onset pelvic pain and severe dysuria. Physical examination reveals bruising on the inner thighs and fresh lacerations of the labia majora. The patient is distressed, crying, and unable to explain what happened. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line clinical management?', '["Treat empirically for a urinary tract infection (UTI) with oral ciprofloxacin and return the patient to the facility without further workup", "Prescribe an oral benzodiazepine to reduce the patient''s distress and recommend a pelvic ultrasound in 3 months", "Confront the administrator of the assisted living facility in the lobby and demand they fire the shift nurse immediately", "Perform a gentle medical-forensic exam (if within the forensic time window), address acute physical injuries, ensure the patient is in a safe environment, and immediately notify law enforcement and the state health department/ombudsman", "Advise the patient''s family to transfer the patient to a different facility immediately to avoid the hassle of an investigation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Unexplained perineal bruising and labial lacerations in a cognitively impaired older adult are highly specific red flags for sexual abuse. The primary clinical steps are: (1) address acute medical needs and ensure safety, (2) perform a medical-forensic examination (SANE/rape kit) to collect evidence, (3) notify law enforcement immediately, and (4) report the facility to the state health department and long-term care ombudsman, as institutional settings require administrative investigations. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Lachs & Pillemer, N Engl J Med 2015; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
